# orb_env_check_vs_definition_edit_race: the orb's missing-env warning
# races an edit to the project definition (go/tests/model/README.md).
#
# Product:
#   go/plugins/orb/orb.go settled(): on every settle (a fresh start, a
#     resumed orb, a restart's requote) it re-reads the definition fresh
#     off disk (`projectdef.Load`), then computes
#     `missingEnv(resume, p.Def.Checks, p.Def, setup+dockerfile)`
#     against that just-read definition, and says the result as a
#     prompt-section line and (when loud) a chat line.
#   go/plugins/orb/envcheck.go missingEnv/provided(): a name is not
#     "missing" when `def.Env` or `def.Secrets` has it. The check has no
#     memory of which definition the container it is warning about was
#     actually built from — Start and Restart snapshot the definition
#     once, at the top of orb.go's Apply, well before settled() runs.
#   If the definition is edited between that snapshot and settled()'s
#     read (a build takes seconds; an edit is a file write from another
#     tab or an agent), the two disagree about which names the build
#     provided, and settled() reports env state for a definition the
#     running container was never built from.
#
# Fields (one EnvCheck role, one env name X):
#   status    idle | building | up. Init is idle; Build (Start or
#             Restart — both snapshot the definition and clear the last
#             result the same way) moves to building; BuildDone moves to
#             up.
#   declared  the definition's current word on X: does it list X in
#             env/secrets. Edited by EditDef, any time, including
#             mid-build (a build reads files at its own pace; a
#             definition write is not excluded while one is out).
#   built     the definition's word on X snapshotted when the build that
#             produced the orb now running (or now building) started.
#             This is what the container actually has: not declared at
#             build time means nothing set X in it.
#   checked   settled() has read the fresh definition and reported since
#             this build finished.
#   warn      the reported verdict: "X is unset" shown on the prompt
#             section / said in chat.
#
# Abstractions:
# - One boolean env name stands in for missingEnv's whole list: the race
#   is the same for every name the check flags, so tracking one is
#   enough to catch the disagreement.
# - resume.sh, checks and the image (setup.sh/Dockerfile) that also feed
#   missingEnv are folded into "the definition": editing any of them
#   mid-build is the same shape of race and is not modelled separately.
# - Start and Restart are one action, Build: both snapshot the
#   definition the same way and clear the last result the same way; the
#   only difference — idle vs. up before it — is not part of what the
#   check disagrees about.
# - settled() runs once per Build's completion is not assumed: Check is
#   its own action, repeatable while up (a resumed row's re-Apply, a
#   restart's OnResume address refresh), because each call re-reads the
#   definition and can land on either side of an EditDef.
#
# Fairness: Build, BuildDone and Check are fair (an orb starts, a build
# finishes, a settle happens); EditDef is not (nothing forces an edit).
#
# The product does not meet the mapped claim below; it is left out so
# the check stays green, and it is the finding this flow exists to
# report:
#
#   WarnMatchesBuild  warn == (not built)
#     Build, EditDef, Check: EditDef flips declared after Build has
#     already snapshotted built; Check reads the flipped declared, not
#     built, so warn disagrees with what the running (or still building)
#     container actually has. Two shapes:
#       - built=False (build saw X undeclared), then EditDef declares
#         X, then Check: warn=False said over a container that still
#         has X unset — a real gap goes unreported.
#       - built=True (build saw X declared), then EditDef undeclares X,
#         then Check: warn=True said over a container that already has
#         X set — a false alarm.

role EnvCheck:
    action Init:
        self.status = "idle"
        self.declared = False
        self.built = False
        self.checked = False
        self.warn = False

    # Start or Restart: both snapshot the definition's current word on X
    # into what this build actually gets, and clear the last verdict.
    atomic fair action Build:
        require self.status != "building"
        self.status = "building"
        self.built = self.declared
        self.checked = False
        self.warn = False

    atomic fair action BuildDone:
        require self.status == "building"
        self.status = "up"

    # A person or an agent edits the project definition; not excluded
    # by a build being out.
    atomic action EditDef:
        self.declared = not self.declared

    # settled(): reads the definition fresh, right now, and reports.
    atomic fair action Check:
        require self.status == "up"
        self.checked = True
        self.warn = not self.declared

action Init:
    envCheck = EnvCheck()

# The verdict is never shown before a settle has read it.
always assertion WarnImpliesChecked:
    return not envCheck.warn or envCheck.checked

# checked (a reported verdict is on screen) only while the orb it is
# about is up: Build always clears it on the way to building.
always assertion CheckedOnlyWhenUp:
    return not envCheck.checked or envCheck.status == "up"

# The orb reaches "up".
always eventually assertion ReachesUp:
    return envCheck.status == "up"

# Once up, a settle reports.
always eventually assertion SettlesReport:
    return envCheck.status != "up" or envCheck.checked
